WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.667
This will mark

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:01.935
a significant shift

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.803
in the leadership at the White House.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:04.471
As CNN

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.872
has confirmed, chief of Staff

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:07.307
Ron Klain is expected

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.875
to step down from his post

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.043
in the coming weeks.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:10.844
Sources

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.712
saying that that is expected to happen

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.347
after the State of the Union address,

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.516
which is currently set for February 7th,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.351
but that Clinton would stay on its

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:19.052
for a bit of

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.121
a transition period as they work

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:22.889
towards his replacement.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.624
Now, this comes as Clayne

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.893
has steered the ship at the White House

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.161
since the beginning

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:29.763
of Biden's presidency.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.164
Seen accomplishments

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:32.999
such as steering the country

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.300
and getting COVID.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.970
19 relief passed up on Capitol Hill.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.071
There was also that massive

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.205
infrastructure bill

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.408
as well as invest historic investments

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:42.842
when it comes to climate change

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:43.877
initiatives, of course.

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.545
There have also been a host of challenges

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.679
facing this administration

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.714
that Clayne has overseen,

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.049
including the withdrawal

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:50.417
from Afghanistan.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.085
Some low approval ratings

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.153
for President Biden,

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:54.721
as well as

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.823
the issues involving the economy

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:58.124
and the inflation.

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.592
But this comes

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:00.060
as really

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.029
there has been relatively low turnover

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:04.230
in this White House.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.432
Clean is

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:08.101
perhaps the most high profile official

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:08.968
so far.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:12.572
Who could be leaving in the coming weeks.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:15.208
Of course, this is a very grueling job.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.444
Sources have said that he has talked

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:18.545
for quite some time

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:20.580
about the possibility of stepping down.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.415
It is very rare for a chief of staff

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.150
to stay in this position

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.652
for all four years.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:26.986
And pretty soon,

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.621
the topics of conversation

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:29.789
will start to shift

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.624
on who to who exactly will be

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.459
replacing him.

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:33.960
Officials have quietly been

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.994
speaking about this

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:37.030
in a few potential replacements

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:37.764
on the table

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:38.865
include Steve Ricchetti,

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.934
who's been served as a longtime adviser

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.802
and counselor to the president.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.537
Also, Jeffrey Zients,

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.840
who led the COVID 19 response

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.109
at the White House before leaving

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:50.410
within the last year.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.578
There is also Anita Dunn,

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:52.512
another longtime

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.746
adviser to the president,

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.982
and then to two Cabinet secretaries

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:57.450
who could also be considered.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.651
That's Tom Vilsack

00:01:58.651 --> 00:01:59.853
the agriculture secretary,

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:00.954
and also Marty Walsh,

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:01.955
the labor secretary.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.090
So, so much of the focus

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.459
as the White House is preparing for clean

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:07.560
to possibly leave in

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.728
the coming weeks

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:09.929
will be focused on

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.431
who exactly that replacement will be.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.533
All right.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:13.800
We know

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.801
shortages

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:15.802
of stories between now

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:16.836
and then on who the

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.104
next chief of staff will be

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.739
that that is going to be

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.508
a very interesting story to watch.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.375
Thank you very much.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:22.709
Joining me

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.077
now, a former Republican congressman

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:25.712
from Pennsylvania, Charlie Dent,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:26.913
and Democratic strategist

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.314
and former Clinton White House aide,

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.216
Keith Boykin. Keith.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.553
This kind of turnover is not anything new

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.054
when it comes to administrations.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.789
We've seen this happen time and again.

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.057
What do you make of this news

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:38.691
and the impact

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.026
it will have on the administration?

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:42.128
I mean, you can't dismiss it too much.

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:43.730
Ron Klain is a

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.231
a very big deal in this Biden

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.299
White House.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.401
Yeah, you're right, Jim.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.869
And I do like Ron Klain.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.237
I think he's been a very effective

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:53.106
chief of staff for the White House.

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:54.874
And he's helped to steer

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:56.509
through important legislation

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.377
through Congress

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.878
got the COVID relief bill,

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.280
the infrastructure bill,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.014
the Inflation

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.616
Reduction Act, the prescription drugs

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:04.951
reduction, you know, all these things

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:05.785
that are really important.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:07.153
It wouldn't have happened without Ron

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.254
Klain's leadership.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:09.289
He's someone who was admired

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:10.490
and respected for some time.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.024
I worked with him

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.292
back in the Clinton administration

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:13.359
back in the nineties.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.527
So it's going to be hard to

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.361
fill his shoes,

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.130
I think, to be able to replace him

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.331
But having said that,

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.433
you know, I think this is not unusual.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.067
As you said, it's

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.602
not surprising to have a turnover

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:24.170
at this point after two years.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:24.771
At this point

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:26.039
in the Trump administration.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:26.673
I think they're already

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.708
on their third chief of staff.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:30.410
So having one chief of staff last

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.412
this long is pretty impressive.

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:33.112
And I'm not sure who

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.714
the replacement will be,

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:35.415
but I'm hoping

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:35.815
that'll be

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.784
someone who is not a white man, honestly,

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:39.452
because every chief of staff

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.520
in the history of this country

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.388
has been a white man at the White House.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.189
And it's time,

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:43.890
I think,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.491
for the other 70% of

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.826
the population to be represented

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:47.460
So I think, you know,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.928
people like Anita Dunn and Susan

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.496
Rice and other names I've heard

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.198
are welcome to be

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:53.499
included in the discussion.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:54.734
Yeah.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:55.668
I wonder if Susan Rice

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.370
will come up in this conversation.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.240
I suspect she will in the coming days.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.774
And Charlie, let me ask you this.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.443
I mean, what do you think?

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:04.877
Is this the time

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:05.511
for the president

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:06.279
to make these kinds

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:07.547
of changes on his team?

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:09.649
I mean, he's coming out of this

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:10.049
country

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.351
still in the middle of this controversy

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:13.286
over the classified documents.

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:16.022
And he's weighing this very big decision

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:17.023
about whether or not

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:19.525
he's going to run for reelection.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:20.426
Well, I'm not surprised

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.328
that there is this turnover right now

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:23.329
with Ron Klain.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:23.930
Look, being

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:24.797
chief of staff is,

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:27.400
as it been pointed out, is a brutal job.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.035
They treat you like a pinata,

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:30.103
at least members of Congress.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:30.770
Do they really do

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.305
beat on these chiefs of staff?

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:33.506
It's a tough job.

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:34.307
But, you know,

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:35.041
they're heading into a

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:36.576
likely campaign season.

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:38.077
And when that happens,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.812
look, I can see why there is a change.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:40.046
I mean,

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:40.980
who knows what Ron Klain

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:41.881
would do on the outside?

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:42.749
I'm sure he's going to stay

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:43.850
in the Biden orbit

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.851
and probably very involved

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.352
with his reelection campaign.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.253
So there's going to be moving

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.521
some people around, I suspect.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:49.689
I'm not surprised.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:50.690
It probably makes sense

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.424
if he is, in fact,

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:52.892
going to run for reelection.

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:53.493
But I suspect

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:54.594
the movement was largely due to

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:56.396
the fact that this is a brutal job

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:57.497
and he's done his two years.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:58.598
He has a lot that he can say

00:04:58.598 --> 00:04:59.699
that he accomplished.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:01.367
And and that's why he's moving on.

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:03.936
And, Keith,

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:04.370
this is all

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.371
happening as the White House

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:05.938
is contending

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:07.674
with a Republican controlled House.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:08.775
So the next chief of staff

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:09.542
who comes in as

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.244
is might have a decidedly more

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:12.912
difficult position

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.314
when they become chief of staff.

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:15.548
But the new House

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:16.316
Oversight Committee,

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:18.651
for example, includes some Republicans

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:19.719
who have been pushing

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:20.887
these deranged

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.255
conspiracy theories, Marjorie

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:22.822
Taylor Greene,

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:23.723
who claimed

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:24.757
a plane

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:25.792
or suggested that a plane

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:28.127
did not hit the Pentagon on nine 11.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:28.995
Paul Gosar,

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:29.595
who is appeared

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:31.698
at a white supremacist conference.

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:32.432
Scott Perry,

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.033
who is a key figure in the effort

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:35.501
to help Trump subvert

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.936
the 2020 election results.

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:40.273
Lauren Boebert and so on and so on.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.442
What do you think?

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:45.111
I mean, how does the next chief of staff

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:46.612
how does this

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:47.246
next team, I

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.247
suppose the next chief of staff

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.417
will have his own team or her own team

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:53.419
that comes on board with that person?

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:56.122
How do they handle all this

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.491
Well, I think Ron Klain

00:05:58.491 --> 00:05:59.792
sort of set the model,

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:02.395
even though he wasn't dealing with a

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:03.596
resistant Congress

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:04.397
because Congress

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:06.032
was in the control of the Democrats.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.500
But I think that he kind of

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.234
was a low

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.603
key, low profile chief of staff.

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:10.770
I mean,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.372
everyone in Washington knew him,

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:14.640
but he wasn't on the on the on the shows

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:15.274
all the time.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:16.576
He did his rounds.

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:17.110
We needed to,

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.811
but he wasn't the most visible voice

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.146
in the administration.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:22.081
I think that that's got to be a model.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:23.249
I would think,

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.417
depending on the personality,

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:25.651
the person who follows up.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:27.420
But I would think I'd be a useful model

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.654
for this administration,

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.056
especially given

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.158
the Republican controlled House,

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:32.825
because I think they're going

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:33.960
to self-destruct

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.762
I think it's the job of the White House

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:37.563
to sort of let them self-destruct.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:38.831
You know, from a political point of view,

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.833
let them continue to be obstructionist,

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.168
let them investigate issues

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:43.202
that aren't there.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.138
Let them make fools of themselves

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:46.506
while the White House is busy

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:47.707
doing people's work.

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:48.841
And I think that's the strategy

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.476
that the president is going to be using,

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:52.712
not only effectively for his second

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:54.714
two years of his administration, but for

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:55.381
his campaign.

